infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Two phases, opposite contracts: render vs commit
  desc Render is pure and throwable; commit is the single atomic moment the DOM changes
  compares
    - label Render phase
      children
        - label Pure — no side effects
          desc Same props and state give the same elements; no analytics, network, ref writes, or mutation
        - label Builds a description, not pixels
          desc Cheap immutable element objects — nothing is painted, measured, or touched in the DOM
        - label Interruptible and discardable
          desc Yields ~5 ms inside transitions; a preempted work-in-progress tree is thrown away, not committed
        - label Runs 0, 1, or many times per paint
          desc Repeat-and-discard is exactly why render must stay pure
    - label Commit phase
      children
        - label The only DOM mutation
          desc appendChild, attribute updates, removals applied to the live tree
        - label Atomic and synchronous
          desc One uninterruptible pass — a half-applied DOM is visible corruption
        - label Swaps current and work-in-progress
          desc Double buffering flips the pointer to the freshly built tree
        - label Runs once per committed update
          desc Then layout effects before paint, passive effects after
theme
  palette #6366f1 #f59e0b
